course: dictionary
lesson: 9159
title: Snout: the long nose at the front
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A snout is the long part at the front of an animal's face, where its nose and mouth stick out. Pigs, dogs, crocodiles and anteaters all have snouts. You can work this out from the words around it: «Pigs learn names, solve puzzles and in one study even steered a joystick with their snouts» tells you a snout is something an animal uses to do things.
explain: Scientists look closely at snouts. Their shape can tell you about an animal's food and way of life. «To tell them apart, look at the snout and the teeth: an alligator has a wide U-shaped snout» while a crocodile has a narrower V-shaped one. The shape is a clue, just like the clues in a sentence.
explain: Snout can also be used in a joking or rude way, meaning a person's nose. Some people also use the phrase «to have your snout in the trough» to mean being greedy for money or rewards. Check the other words in the sentence to see which meaning is being used.
fact: A snout is the long nose and mouth part that sticks out at the front of an animal's face.
fact: Pigs, dogs, crocodiles and anteaters all have snouts.
fact: «A sturgeon has... four whiskers under its snout to feel for food.»
fact: In science, the shape of an animal's snout can be an adaptation that helps it find or catch its food.
fact: Informally, and often as a joke, snout can mean a person's nose.
exercise: In «Pigs... steered a joystick with their snouts», which body part is meant? | Its ears | Its tail | Its hooves | *Its long nose and mouth part
exercise: «An alligator has a wide U-shaped snout.» What shape is an alligator's snout? | *Wide and U-shaped | Flat like a leaf | Round like a ball | Narrow and pointed
exercise: «A sturgeon has four whiskers under its snout to feel for food.» What are the whiskers used for? | To fly | *To feel for food | To sing | To keep warm
test: In a joke, what might snout mean for a person? | A hat | *A nose | A shoe | A pair of glasses
test: Why might scientists look at a crocodile's snout shape? | To measure how fast it swims | To check how old it is | To learn its favourite colour | *To see what it eats
exam: «SuperCroc had a round bony bump at the tip of its snout, and nobody knows for sure what it was for.» What does this sentence tell us? | The bump was a kind of tooth | *Scientists are not certain what the bump was for | The bump was definitely for swimming | The bump was used to sing
